content.text:
Confirmed no `nodeStatusOn`/`storageStatusOn`/`listVmsOn` variants exist anywhere in the codebase.

## 1. `lib/monitoring.ts` — `nodeRamUsage()` / `nodeStorageUsage()`: already multi-node aware

Both iterate over `nodeTargets()`, which itself calls `db.node.findMany()`:

```ts
// lines 73-100
export async function nodeTargets(): Promise<PveTarget[]> {
  const nodes = await db.node.findMany({
    where: { enabled: true },
    orderBy: [{ isPrimary: "desc" }, { createdAt: "asc" }],
  });
  if (nodes.length === 0) {
    return [
      {
        label: primaryNode || "primary",
        pveNode: primaryNode,
        get: (p, pp) => pve.get(p, pp),
        storages: ["vmdata", "backup"],
      },
    ];
  }
  return nodes.map((n: Node) => {
    const client = pveFor({
      apiUrl: n.apiUrl,
      pveNode: n.pveNode,
      tokenId: n.tokenId,
      tokenSecret: n.tokenSecret,
    });
    const storages = [n.storageNvme, n.storageHdd, "backup"].filter(
      (v, i, a) => v && a.indexOf(v) === i
    );
    return { label: n.name, pveNode: n.pveNode, get: client.get, storages };
  });
}
```

```ts
// lines 113-138
export async function nodeStorageUsage(): Promise<StorageUsage[]> {
  const targets = await nodeTargets();
  const out: StorageUsage[] = [];
  for (const t of targets) {
    for (const storage of t.storages) {
      try {
        const st = await t.get(`/nodes/${t.pveNode}/storage/${storage}/status`);
        ...
        out.push({ node: t.label, storage, total, used, avail, ratio: total > 0 ? used / total : 0, reachable: true });
      } catch {
        out.push({ node: t.label, storage, total: 0, used: 0, avail: 0, ratio: 0, reachable: false });
      }
    }
  }
  return out;
}
```

```ts
// lines 152-176
export async function nodeRamUsage(): Promise<NodeRam[]> {
  const targets = await nodeTargets();
  return Promise.all(
    targets.map(async (t) => {
      try {
        const s = await t.get(`/nodes/${t.pveNode}/status`);
        ...
        return { node: t.label, total, used, ratio: total > 0 ? used / total : 0, cpu: Number(s?.cpu ?? 0), cores: Number(s?.cpuinfo?.cpus ?? 0), reachable: true };
      } catch {
        return { node: t.label, total: 0, used: 0, ratio: 0, cpu: 0, cores: 0, reachable: false };
      }
    })
  );
}
```

Both functions, and `backupSummary()`/`proxmoxHealth()` in the same file, fan out over every enabled `Node` row via `pveFor(n)`, falling back to the single-node singleton only when `db.node` is empty. They are already fully multi-node aware — nothing to change here.

## 2. `app/admin/page.tsx` — calls the bare singleton functions directly

```ts
// line 4
import { nodeStatus, storageStatus } from "@/lib/proxmox";
```

```ts
// lines 110-122
// Live hypervisor + storage (graceful fallback)
let node: any = null;
let vmdata: any = null;
let backup: any = null;
try {
  [node, vmdata, backup] = await Promise.all([
    nodeStatus(),
    storageStatus("vmdata"),
    storageStatus("backup"),
  ]);
} catch {
  // Proxmox unreachable — cards render a fallback below
}
```

These are rendered in two single-node cards: "Hypervisor health" (CPU/memory/uptime/load/kernel from `node`, lines 191-240) and "Storage pools" (`vmdata`/`backup` used/total, lines 242-287). Neither the queries nor the rendered JSX reference `db.node` at all — this page is hardcoded to whatever the env-configured primary (`PROXMOX_NODE`/`PROXMOX_API_URL`) is, and will show only that one node no matter how many `Node` rows exist. This is the piece that needs to change for multi-node support (e.g., swap to `nodeRamUsage()`/`nodeStorageUsage()`/`nodeTargets()` from `lib/monitoring.ts` and render a card per node).

## 3. `app/api/metrics/route.ts` — admin/no-serverId branch

```ts
// line 4
import { vmRrd, nodeRrd, pveFor } from "@/lib/proxmox";
```

```ts
// lines 39-43
if (user.role !== "ADMIN") {
  return NextResponse.json({ error: "forbidden" }, { status: 403, headers: NO_STORE_HEADERS });
}
const data = await nodeRrd(timeframe);
return NextResponse.json({ data }, { headers: NO_STORE_HEADERS });
```

`nodeRrd(timeframe)` (from `proxmox.ts`, line 506-508) is the bare singleton call — `pve.get(/nodes/${NODE}/rrddata`, ...)` — always against the env `NODE`/primary. `db.node` is NOT referenced anywhere in this file at all (only `db.server` is used, in the `serverId` branch at line 22, to resolve a per-VM `pveFor(...)` connection via `server.node`). So the admin fleet-wide RRD branch has the same single-node limitation as the admin dashboard page — it never looks at `db.node.findMany()`.

## Proxmox singleton signatures (`lib/proxmox.ts`)

All three take no connection/client parameter — hardcoded to the module-level `NODE`/`pve` singleton (env `PROXMOX_NODE`/`PROXMOX_API_URL`/token):

```ts
// line 489-491
export async function nodeStatus(): Promise<any> {
  return pve.get(`/nodes/${NODE}/status`);
}

// line 493-495
export async function listVms(): Promise<any[]> {
  return pve.get(`/nodes/${NODE}/qemu`);
}

// line 937-939
export async function storageStatus(storage: string): Promise<any> {
  return pve.get(`/nodes/${NODE}/storage/${storage}/status`);
}
```

No `nodeStatusOn`/`storageStatusOn`/`listVmsOn` conn-aware variants exist (confirmed via grep across the whole tree — zero matches). The codebase's established pattern for "same call, but on a specific node's connection" instead uses a `client: any`/`TaskClient`/`PveClient` first-parameter convention, seen in the sibling functions that DO already have both a singleton and an "On" variant:

- `listSnapshots(vmid)` (singleton, line 685-688) vs `listSnapshotsOn(client, vmid)` (line 694-697)
- `listBackups(vmid?)` (singleton, line 699-702) vs `listBackupsOn(client, vmid)` (line 810-816)
- `vmRrd(vmid, timeframe, conn?: PveClient)` (line 500-504) already takes an **optional** third `conn` param and falls back to `pve`/`NODE` when omitted — this is the pattern `metrics/route.ts` already leans on for the per-server branch (via `pveFor(server.node...)`).

So `nodeStatus`, `storageStatus`, and `listVms` are the ones missing the "On"/optional-conn variant that the rest of the file already establishes as the multi-node convention. Adding `nodeStatusOn(client)`, `storageStatusOn(client, storage)`, `listVmsOn(client)` (or giving the existing functions an optional `conn?: PveClient` parameter like `vmRrd` has) would match existing conventions and is what `nodeTargets()` in `monitoring.ts` already needs — note `nodeTargets()` doesn't even call these `proxmox.ts` functions; it builds its own `t.get(...)` calls inline instead, which is a separate small inconsistency worth knowing about (the admin dashboard page, once fixed, could either follow `monitoring.ts`'s ad-hoc-client approach or use new `On` variants of `nodeStatus`/`storageStatus`/`listVms`).
